// T125 2026-09-06: `&&` and `||` pinned. REDERIVED 2026-09-14 for A26 (compiler half
// `0cb2c23`): they are REAL LOGICAL OPERATORS now -- value 0/1, both operands through
// `vs_to_bool`, still NO short circuit (that half of T125 is unchanged) -- and their
// precedence is `|| < && < cmp` (`bebop.bp:3853`), LOOSER than comparison rather than
// tighter.
//
// The old header said 111100 and that number was the PRE-A26 MIS-PARSE, not a measurement
// of anything correct. Both readings derived by hand with a = 3, b = 5, and `q` is the only
// term that moves:
//   post-A26 (correct): q = (3>5) && (5<9) = 0 && 1 = 0
//   pre-A26           : `&&` was `&` and bound tighter, so `a > b && b < 9` parsed as
//                       `a > (b & b) < 9` = `(3>5) < 9` = `0 < 9` = 1
//   post-A26 total: 1*100000 + 0*10000 + 1*1000 + 0*100 + 100 = 101100
//   pre-A26  total: 1*100000 + 1*10000 + 1*1000 + 0*100 + 100 = 111100
// bebop.bin printed 101100 all along; `tools/bpref.py` printed 111100 because it still had
// `&&`/`||` aliased to `&`/`|` in the `&`/`|` tiers, so the oracle AGREED WITH THE STALE
// HEADER and the two implementations genuinely disagreed. bpref was brought to A26 in the
// same commit as this header; `BPREF_PREA26=1` still reproduces 111100.
// EXPECT 101100 from: hand: logical AND/OR (A26; q is the discriminating term)
fn main() -> i64 {
  let a = 3;
  let b = 5;
  let p = if a < b && b < 9 then 1 else 0;
  let q = if a > b && b < 9 then 1 else 0;
  let r = if a > b || b < 9 then 1 else 0;
  let s = if a > b || b > 9 then 1 else 0;
  let t = (a < b && b < 9) * 100 + (a > b || b > 9) * 10;
  p * 100000 + q * 10000 + r * 1000 + s * 100 + t
}
